To generate an automatic table of contents (TOC) in a LaTeX document, you can use the `toc` package and its `\tableofcontents` command. Here's an example of how to do it:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[english]{babel}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{graphicx}

% Define the TOC style
\makeatletter
\renewcommand{\cftsecleader}{\quad}
\renewcommand{\cftsecafter}{\addpenalty{-1000\p@} \addvspace*{\parskip}}
\makeatother

% Generate the TOC
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\tableofcontents

% Add sections with subsections
\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Section 1}
\subsubsection{Subsection 1.1}
\subsubsection{Subsection 1.2}
\subsubsection{Subsection 1.3}

\end{document}
```

In this example, we first define the TOC style using the `\makeatletter` and `\makeatother` commands from the `toc` package. We then redefine the `\cftsecleader`, `\cftsecafter`, and `\cftsectnum` commands to add space between sections.

Next, we use the `\maketitle` command to create a title for our document. Then, we use the `\tableofcontents` command to generate the TOC.

Finally, we add two sections (`Introduction` and `Section 1`) with their respective sub-sections (`Subsection 1.1`, `Subsection 1.2`, and `Subsection 1.3`). Each section is indented by one tab stop (`\addpenalty{-1000\p@}`), which ensures that each section has its own TOC entry.

When you compile this LaTeX document, you should see a list of all the sections and their sub-sections listed under the TOC heading. You can also customize the appearance of the TOC by modifying the `\cftsecleader`, `\cftsecafter`, and `\cftsectnum` commands as needed.

